  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 341 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nTuesday through Sunday:\n\nHeat and humidity builds across southern Wisconsin Tuesday, with\nheat index values in the mid to upper 90s expected. Highest\ntemperatures are expected near the WI/IL border, and a few spots\nmay break 100 degree heat index values with temperatures in the\nupper 80s. However, overrunning precipitation from the previous\nnight, should it develop, could temper these high temperatures \nand prevent Heat Advisory criteria from being met. Southwesterly\nwinds will allow for heat to make it all the way to Lake \nMichigan, although afternoon lake breeze influence may offer \nsome relief. Overnight Tuesday, LLJ dynamics return to the area,\nwith widespread precipitation possible in the WAA region across\ncentral Wisconsin to northern Iowa. If this precipitation is \nable to form a cold pool, severe weather would become possible \nas an MCS moves eastward through the mean flow. Kept NBM PoPs of\n20-40%.\n\nIsolated storms may linger into Wednesday, but with building\nridging not anticipating significant impacts. The more impactful\nstory at this point will be the heat index values in the upper\n90s to potentially 100 degrees near the IL border where air\ntemperatures reach 90 degrees. Cloud cover remaining across \ncentral WI will keep heat concerns lower, with indices near 90 \ndegrees and temperatures in the mid-80s. Wednesday night, LLJ\nfocuses on central Iowa, which indicates a decaying MCS may\nmove eastward through southern Wisconsin into Thursday morning.\nGoing through the rest of the workweek, continued periods of\non-and-off thunderstorms are expected as southern Wisconsin\nremains along the northern edge of ridging across the Southeast\nand Mississippi Valley regions. Exact details will be refined as\nevents approach. \n\nPattern looks to dry out into next weekend as a drier airmass\nfrom northeastern Canada works its way southward into the Great\nLakes region.\n\nMH",
